@prefix censo:   <https://w3id.org/censo/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix vann:    <http://purl.org/vocab/vann/> .
@prefix sosa:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix ssn:     <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix qudt:    <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix foaf:    <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .

################################################################################
#  CENSO — Censored ENvironmental Sensor Observations
#  v1.0.0 — first public release
#
#  A domain-independent extension of SOSA/SSN for observations produced by
#  analytical methods that have a detection limit.
#
#  THE PROBLEM
#  -----------
#  sosa:Observation carries sosa:hasSimpleResult, a point value. The standard has
#  no notion of a detection limit, so it cannot express the most common outcome
#  in trace-level monitoring:
#
#      "the substance was looked for and not found"
#
#  which asserts an UPPER BOUND, not a value, and is an OPEN-WORLD statement: the
#  substance may be present below the limit. Recording it as 0 forces open-world
#  data into a closed-world model. The information destroyed is the MODALITY, not
#  the magnitude — a type error, not a rounding convention.
#
#  A threshold, correspondingly, is not a number. It is a conditional judgement
#  with preconditions (matrix, analytical fraction, speciation or bioavailability
#  basis, required covariates, regulation version). Where the preconditions are
#  unmet, compliance is undecidable even though both a value and a threshold
#  exist. Existing models keep the number and discard the preconditions, so they
#  report compliance for cases in which no assessment is possible.
#
#  WHAT THE AXIOMS DO
#  ------------------
#  The axioms below are chosen to CATCH ERRORS, not to decorate. Specifically:
#    * detection statuses are disjoint AND exhaustive, so an observation asserted
#      both censored and quantified makes the ontology inconsistent;
#    * the three compliance outcomes are exhaustive, and exclusive PER
#      OBSERVATION-THRESHOLD PAIR rather than per observation: two verdicts about
#      the same pair are a contradiction, two verdicts about two jurisdictions'
#      thresholds are not. The subclasses of the third outcome record WHY it was
#      reached and are deliberately NOT disjoint from one another, for the same
#      reason;
#    * owl:hasKey makes duplicate observations detectable;
#    * minimum-cardinality restrictions on analyte, unit, procedure and on a
#      threshold's value, unit and source document state what a usable record
#      MUST carry. Read them as the specification, not as the enforcement: under
#      the open world assumption a missing value is unknown, not absent, so a
#      minimum cardinality ENTAILS the existence of a filler and never rejects
#      anything. It is censo-shapes.ttl that rejects, because SHACL closes the
#      world locally, which is precisely why the shapes ship beside the axioms
#      rather than duplicating them.
#  The disjointness, exhaustiveness and hasKey axioms above DO reject: each one
#  fires on a defect that was actually present in the dataset this ontology was
#  built from, and scripts/test_axioms.py removes the named axiom to show the
#  case stops failing without it.
#
#  WHAT OWL CANNOT DO HERE — stated plainly
#  ----------------------------------------
#  1. OWL cannot compare two data property values. "resultLowerBound greater than
#     thresholdValue" is not expressible. The comparison is therefore performed
#     by the rule layer, which MATERIALISES censo:exceeds / censo:possiblyExceeds
#     / censo:belowThreshold. OWL then classifies over those object properties.
#     We do not claim a DL reasoner computes compliance.
#  2. Under the open-world assumption, "none of the other three outcomes applies"
#     cannot be inferred, because an unasserted property is not a false one. This
#     is the same OWA that makes CENSO right for censoring, and it cuts both
#     ways: censo:IndeterminateCompliance must be MATERIALISED under a locally
#     closed world (SHACL, or a rule with negation-as-failure over a completed
#     assessment), not inferred by subsumption. See censo-shapes.ttl.
#     The covering axiom below still earns its place: once an outcome is
#     asserted, it forces exactly one, and any second assertion is a clash.
#
#  NUMERIC TYPE
#  ------------
#  Concentrations, limits and thresholds are xsd:decimal, not xsd:double.
#  Regulatory limits are published as exact decimals and compliance turns on
#  exact comparison; binary floating point introduces representation error into
#  a legal judgement. Decimal also avoids the lexical-form ambiguity that makes
#  double literals fragile across RDF toolchains.
#
#  APPLICABILITY
#  -------------
#  Any measurement process with a detection limit: water and air quality, food
#  safety residues, clinical assays, soil contaminants, occupational exposure.
################################################################################

censo: a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:title "CENSO: an ontology for censored environmental observations"@en ;
    dcterms:description """CENSO extends SOSA/SSN with detection and quantification
limits, interval-valued results, applicability conditions on regulatory
thresholds, and a three-valued compliance assessment in which "cannot be
determined" is an explicit, queryable outcome, subtyped by the reason it cannot. Designed for measurement
processes in which non-detection is the normal result and must be represented as
an open-world upper bound rather than as zero."""@en ;
    dcterms:creator [ a foaf:Person ; foaf:name "Recep Can Altınbağ" ] ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-03"^^xsd:date ;
    vann:preferredNamespacePrefix "censo" ;
    vann:preferredNamespaceUri "https://w3id.org/censo/" ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/censo/1.0.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> ;
    owl:imports <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> ,
                <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> ,
                <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> ,
                <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core> .

################################################################################
#  1. Analyte
################################################################################

censo:Analyte a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Analyte"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A substance whose concentration is the observed property.

An Analyte is NOT the substance. It is a sosa:ObservableProperty — the
concentration OF a substance — while a chemical ontology's class is the
substance itself, whose instances are molecules. The distinction decides how
the two are related: ontology/censo-alignment.ttl points each analyte at its
ChEBI class with rdfs:seeAlso, and reserves owl:sameAs for the relation that IS
an identity, between two regulation packages' names for one observable
property. That is what lets a European and a Turkish package resolve to one
thing, which is the precondition for comparing them.

This comment used to claim the alignment was made with skos:exactMatch. It was
made with nothing at all: the only occurrence of skos:exactMatch in the whole
ontology was inside this sentence, and the comparison worked by joining CAS
strings in Python. The alignment module now exists and the audit checks that
every substance the dual-regulation analysis treats as co-regulated is
reconciled by it."""@en .

censo:casNumber a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Analyte ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "CAS registry number"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Preferred join key across heterogeneous sources; more
reliable than trade or trivial names.

NOT owl:FunctionalProperty, and the correction is worth recording. It was
declared functional, and the released EU package violates that on three
entries, because Annex I names more than one registry number for a single
regulated substance: diclofenac as the free acid (15307-86-5) and as the sodium
salt (15307-79-6), and likewise acetamiprid and imidacloprid. A regulatory
entry is not a single chemical species, and the data were right.

The violation was invisible. A functional datatype property with two distinct
literals on one subject makes the ontology inconsistent under DL semantics, but
OWL 2 RL derives owl:sameAs between the literals and stops there, so owlrl
reports no owl:Nothing and the audit's package-consistency check passed on every
run. scripts/99_audit.py::check_functional_properties now looks directly."""@en .

################################################################################
#  2. Analytical method and its limits
################################################################################

censo:AnalyticalMethod a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Procedure ;
    rdfs:label "Analytical method"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A procedure determining the concentration of an analyte,
characterised by a limit of detection and a limit of quantification. The limits
belong to the METHOD, not to the substance: the same analyte may be censored at
one laboratory and quantified at another. Attaching limits to the analyte — the
common shortcut — makes cross-laboratory comparison unsound."""@en ;
    skos:definition "A sosa:Procedure with a detection limit."@en .

################################################################################
#  2a. Where the limit belongs, in three steps
#
#  SOSA/SSN was designed for SENSING: a device observes in situ, continuously,
#  with a fixed measurement range. That is why ssn-system:DetectionLimit is a
#  ssn-system:SystemProperty -- for a sensor the limit really is a permanent
#  property of the instrument.
#
#  Trace organic monitoring is not sensing. It is sampling: a specimen is taken
#  at one place and instant, transported, preserved, and analysed later by a
#  method with its own calibration and its own limits for that analyte and that
#  matrix. So the limit is a property of the METHOD, not of a device.
#
#  That is where CHMO, the OBO Chemical Methods Ontology, puts it, and CHMO is
#  the closest prior art there is: it declares `limit of detection` and `limit of
#  quantification` from the IUPAC Gold Book and types them as a `figure of merit`
#  of an assay. A better place than a sensor -- and still not a place from which
#  any particular result can be called censored, because nothing in CHMO relates
#  a limit to a result.
#
#  THE THIRD STEP IS THE CONTRIBUTION. The limit has to be CARRIED ONTO THE
#  RESULT, as censo:resultUpperBound on the observation itself, because
#  "censored" is a property of a measurement and not of the procedure that
#  produced it. Two observations from the same method, one below the limit and
#  one above, differ in exactly this and in nothing else. A limit held on the
#  method describes what could have been detected; a bound held on the result
#  says what this measurement established.
#
#  So: sensor (SSN-system) -> method (CHMO) -> result (here). The gap table's
#  `LOD bound to` column is that sequence, and it is the discriminating column.
#
#  PER-RUN LIMITS, AND WHY THERE IS NO CLASS FOR THEM
#  --------------------------------------------------
#  Limits vary between analytical runs: the same method recalibrated tomorrow
#  has different ones. Earlier versions of this file declared
#  censo:AnalyticalRun, censo:producedByRun and censo:runUsedMethod for that,
#  and they are retired, for two reasons.
#
#  First, they were redundant with a vocabulary this file already imports. A run
#  is a prov:Activity; it prov:used a sample and the observation
#  prov:wasGeneratedBy it, and the method it executed carries the limits through
#  sosa:usedProcedure. Declaring censo: terms for that reinvents PROV-O.
#
#  Second, and this is the harder reason: no European monitoring release reports
#  a run identifier, and minting one per aggregated station-year would be
#  FALSE -- an annual mean spans many runs, so asserting that it was produced by
#  one is not a simplification but a wrong statement. The shapes in this project
#  were already corrected once for a constraint that could only be satisfied by
#  manufacturing its own evidence (a detection limit invented at LOQ/3). A class
#  instantiated the same way would repeat that mistake, and this vocabulary now
#  fails its own build on any term that no shipped graph exercises
#  (scripts/99_audit.py, check_no_dead_terms).
#
#  What the vocabulary does instead is honest about the granularity it has: the
#  limit is asserted on the method and the bound it produces is asserted on the
#  result. Where a publisher does have run-level provenance, PROV expresses it
#  with no extension needed, and censo:resultUpperBound is where its consequence
#  lands either way.
################################################################################




censo:limitOfDetection a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:AnalyticalMethod ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "limit of detection (LOD)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Lowest concentration reliably distinguishable from a blank.
Below it, presence can be neither asserted nor denied. Expressed in
censo:limitUnit."""@en .

censo:limitOfQuantification a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:AnalyticalMethod ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "limit of quantification (LOQ)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lowest concentration measurable with stated accuracy and precision."@en .

censo:limitUnit a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:AnalyticalMethod ;
    rdfs:range qudt:Unit ;
    rdfs:label "limit unit"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Unit of LOD and LOQ. Mandatory: a bare limit is not comparable to anything."@en .

censo:determinesAnalyte a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:AnalyticalMethod ;
    rdfs:range censo:Analyte ;
    rdfs:label "determines analyte"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The analyte this method measures. One method may cover many analytes, each with its own limits, so limits are asserted per method-analyte pair."@en .

censo:calibrationR2 a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:AnalyticalMethod ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "calibration coefficient of determination"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "R² of the calibration curve; a quality indicator for the reported limits."@en .

# A method with no unit cannot support any censoring statement: a bare limit is
# not comparable to anything, and the unit is the one datum every release does
# carry. The restriction is stated here.
#
# WHICH LIMIT IS REQUIRED, AND WHY IT IS NOT STATED HERE.
# This block used to require exactly one censo:limitOfDetection as well. No
# European monitoring release reports one -- WISE-6 has a field for the
# quantification limit and none for the detection limit -- and the legal tests
# the vocabulary is built to express are written about the QUANTIFICATION limit
# (Art. 3(3b) of 2008/105/EC, Art. 4(1) of 2009/90/EC). So the axiom demanded a
# datum that does not exist, and the only graph that ever satisfied it did so by
# manufacturing it at LOD = LOQ/3. censo-shapes.ttl was relaxed to require *a*
# limit and this was not, which left the shipped ABox contradicting its own TBox
# in 1,159 methods while both validators looked straight past it: OWL 2 RL
# ignores a minimum cardinality in superclass position, and the relaxed shape
# accepts the LOQ alone.
#
# The requirement that survives is a DISJUNCTION -- at least one of LOD, LOQ --
# and owl:unionOf in superclass position is outside OWL 2 RL, the profile this
# ontology commits to. It therefore lives where it can actually be enforced, as
# censo:AnalyticalMethodLimitShape in censo-shapes.ttl. This is the same division of
# labour the header states for censo:IndeterminateCompliance: what OWL cannot
# decide is not asserted in OWL and pretended to hold.
censo:AnalyticalMethod rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:limitUnit ;
      owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] .

################################################################################
#  3. Detection status — the epistemic core
#
#  Disjoint AND exhaustive over observations that were actually made.
#
#  The absence of an observation individual is a FIFTH, distinct situation
#  ("never measured") and is deliberately NOT modelled as a class: under the open
#  world assumption, not asserting an observation is exactly the right way to say
#  it was not made. This is the distinction a `WHERE concentration = 0` query in
#  a closed-world store cannot make, and conflating it with non-detection
#  manufactures spurious findings.
################################################################################

censo:CensoredObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "Censored observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The analyte was sought and not detected. Asserts only that the
true concentration lies in [0, LOD]. It does NOT assert absence. Storing such an
observation as 0 destroys the distinction and biases every derived aggregate
downwards."""@en ;
    skos:definition "Observation whose result is below the method's limit of detection."@en ;
    skos:example "Atrazine sought and not detected by a method whose limit of detection is 0.02 µg/L: the concentration lies in [0, 0.02], which is not the same claim as zero."@en .

censo:EstimatedObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "Estimated observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Detected, but between LOD and LOQ, so the reported magnitude
is indicative only. Reporting it as a point estimate overstates what the method
supports. Datasets frequently contain such values presented as if quantified."""@en .

censo:QuantifiedObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "Quantified observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Detected at or above the limit of quantification; the magnitude is usable."@en .

censo:UnresolvedObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "Unresolved observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Made, but its detection status cannot be established, typically
because the applicable LOD/LOQ for the analyte-method pair is unknown. Such
observations are structurally undecidable and must be excluded from inference
rather than silently treated as quantified."""@en .

censo:AssessedObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "Assessed observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An observation whose detection status has been determined; the union of the four statuses."@en ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
        owl:unionOf ( censo:CensoredObservation censo:EstimatedObservation
                      censo:QuantifiedObservation censo:UnresolvedObservation ) ] .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    owl:members ( censo:CensoredObservation censo:EstimatedObservation
                  censo:QuantifiedObservation censo:UnresolvedObservation ) .

# Detected = estimated or quantified: the analyte was found, whether or not a
# number could be attached to it. The union is worth naming because presence is
# far more robust than magnitude near the limit -- an estimated result carries a
# concentration nobody should compare against a standard, and yet it settles the
# question of whether the substance was there. Reasoning that turns on presence
# rather than on magnitude therefore operates on this class. (The spatial
# detection-onset analysis this comment used to cite is not part of the work
# reported alongside this vocabulary; the class stands on its own.)
censo:DetectedObservation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Detected observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The analyte was found, whether or not it could be quantified."@en ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
        owl:unionOf ( censo:EstimatedObservation censo:QuantifiedObservation ) ] .

censo:DetectedObservation owl:disjointWith censo:CensoredObservation .

################################################################################
#  4. Interval-valued results
#
#  Censoring makes a result an interval. The bounds are MATERIALISED by the rule
#  layer from the reported value and the method limits; OWL classifies over them.
################################################################################

censo:resultLowerBound a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "result lower bound"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.w3.org/TR/xmlschema11-2/#decimal> ;
    rdfs:comment "Smallest concentration consistent with the observation; 0 when censored."@en .

censo:resultUpperBound a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "result upper bound"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Largest concentration consistent with the observation; the LOD when censored."@en .

censo:reportedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "reported value"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The number as it appears in the source record, kept verbatim for
provenance. Never use it directly in inference: in many datasets a reported 0 is
a non-detection, not a measured zero."""@en .

censo:hasAnalyte a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Analyte ;
    rdfs:label "has analyte"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The substance this observation is about; exactly one."@en .

censo:atStation a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range sosa:FeatureOfInterest ;
    rdfs:label "at station"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the sample was taken."@en .

censo:duringCampaign a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Campaign ;
    rdfs:label "during campaign"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The sampling campaign the observation belongs to; part of the observation's identity key."@en .

censo:Campaign a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Sampling campaign"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A bounded sampling exercise. Making it explicit lets the same station be compared across time and prevents campaigns being conflated."@en .

# IDENTITY. An observation is uniquely determined by analyte, station and
# campaign. Two individuals agreeing on all three are the same observation, so
# duplicated rows in a source spreadsheet become detectable rather than
# double-counted.
sosa:Observation owl:hasKey ( censo:hasAnalyte censo:atStation censo:duringCampaign ) .

# An observation with no analyte, no station or no procedure is not
# interpretable; these restrictions reject it instead of carrying it forward.
censo:AssessedObservation rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:hasAnalyte ;
      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
      owl:onClass censo:Analyte ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:atStation ;
      owl:minCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty sosa:usedProcedure ;
      owl:minCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] .

# A censored observation asserts an upper bound and nothing more; its lower
# bound is necessarily zero.
censo:CensoredObservation rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:resultLowerBound ;
      owl:hasValue "0.0"^^xsd:decimal ] .

################################################################################
#  5. Thresholds as conditional judgements
################################################################################

censo:Threshold a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A concentration limit set by a regulation, together with the conditions under which it may lawfully be applied."@en .

censo:AnnualAverageThreshold a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:label "Annual average threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Long-term limit (e.g. AA-EQS). Applies to a temporal mean, not
to a single sample; comparing one grab sample against it is a category error that
this class exists to make visible."""@en .

censo:MaximumAllowableThreshold a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:label "Maximum allowable threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Short-term limit (e.g. MAC-EQS); may be compared against a single observation."@en .

# Two members, not three. censo:ClassBoundaryThreshold was retired: it named a
# real distinction -- conventional determinands are assessed against quality
# class boundaries, not against an EQS -- but no regulation package this work
# ships declares one, so the class was a promise rather than a term. The axiom
# that matters is unchanged: an annual mean and a maximum allowable limit are
# different kinds of judgement, and scripts/test_axioms.py T8 fires on a graph
# that conflates them.
[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    owl:members ( censo:AnnualAverageThreshold
                  censo:MaximumAllowableThreshold ) .

censo:thresholdValue a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "threshold value"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The numeric limit, meaningful only with its unit and applicability conditions."@en .

censo:thresholdUnit a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:range qudt:Unit ;
    rdfs:label "threshold unit"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Unit of the threshold; must be commensurable with the observation unit before comparison."@en .

censo:appliesToAnalyte a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:range censo:Analyte ;
    rdfs:label "applies to analyte"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The substance constrained. Group thresholds use cereg:appliesToGroup instead."@en .

censo:definedBy a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:range censo:Regulation ;
    rdfs:label "defined by"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The legal instrument establishing the threshold. Mandatory: an uncited threshold cannot be defended."@en .

censo:Regulation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Regulation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A dated, versioned legal instrument. Thresholds must cite one, because they change."@en .

# A threshold without a value, a unit or a citation is not usable.
censo:Threshold rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:thresholdValue ;
      owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:thresholdUnit ;
      owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:definedBy ;
      owl:cardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ] .

# ---- preconditions -----------------------------------------------------------

censo:ApplicabilityCondition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Applicability condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A condition that must hold before a threshold may be applied to an observation."@en .

censo:requiresCondition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:range censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:label "requires condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A precondition of the threshold. Unmet preconditions yield no verdict, not a default pass."@en .

censo:MatrixCondition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:label "Matrix condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Restricts the threshold to a matrix: inland surface water, coastal water, biota, sediment."@en .

censo:BioavailabilityCondition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:label "Bioavailability condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The threshold is a bioavailable concentration (as for nickel,
lead, copper and zinc under EU EQS) and requires hardness, dissolved organic
carbon and pH for normalisation. Absent those covariates, no compliance
statement is possible."""@en .

censo:HardnessClassCondition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:label "Hardness class condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The threshold varies by water hardness class, as for cadmium.
Without a hardness measurement the applicable class — and therefore the
applicable threshold — is unknown."""@en .

censo:requiresCovariate a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:range sosa:ObservableProperty ;
    rdfs:label "requires covariate"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An observable property that must be co-measured for the condition to be evaluable."@en .

censo:conditionSatisfied a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:ApplicabilityCondition ;
    rdfs:label "condition satisfied"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Materialised by the rule layer when the observation demonstrably meets the condition."@en .

censo:assessableAgainst a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:label "assessable against"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Materialised when EVERY condition the threshold requires is
satisfied for this observation. Only then may a verdict be drawn. Universal
quantification over a materialised set is a closed-world check, so it lives in
SHACL, not in OWL."""@en .

################################################################################
#  6. Three-valued compliance, subtyped by the reason
#
#  Exhaustive. Materialisation of the three comparison properties is
#  done by the rule layer (OWL cannot compare two data values); OWL then forces
#  exactly one outcome and reports a clash if a pipeline ever asserts two.
################################################################################

censo:exceeds a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf censo:assessableAgainst ;
    rdfs:label "exceeds"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Materialised when resultLowerBound > thresholdValue: exceedance holds for every value consistent with the observation."@en .

censo:possiblyExceeds a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf censo:assessableAgainst ;
    rdfs:label "possibly exceeds"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Materialised when resultLowerBound <= thresholdValue < resultUpperBound: the interval straddles the limit."@en .

censo:belowThreshold a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range censo:Threshold ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf censo:assessableAgainst ;
    rdfs:label "below threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Materialised when resultUpperBound <= thresholdValue."@en .

# The three comparisons are mutually exclusive by construction; asserting two of
# them for the same observation-threshold pair is a pipeline bug, and this makes
# it an inconsistency instead of a silent contradiction.
#
# THIS is where the outcome logic is exclusive. Not at the class level: a
# compliance outcome belongs to an observation-threshold PAIR, and the same
# measurement may be compliant against one jurisdiction's standard and
# indeterminate against another's. Disjointness here forbids two verdicts about
# one pair and permits different verdicts about different thresholds, which is
# exactly the distinction the multi-regulation assessment needs.
[] a owl:AllDisjointProperties ;
    owl:members ( censo:exceeds censo:possiblyExceeds censo:belowThreshold ) .

# The same statement, pairwise. owl:AllDisjointProperties(p q r) and the three
# owl:propertyDisjointWith axioms are equivalent in OWL 2, so this adds no
# semantics -- but OWL 2 RL rule prp-adp is not implemented by owlrl, the pure
# Python engine this pipeline uses, while prp-pdw is. Without these three
# triples the axiom is asserted and never checked: scripts/test_axioms.py T4
# passed only because a class-level disjointness axiom happened to catch the
# same case, and when that axiom was removed the test went green-to-red and
# showed that per-pair exclusivity had never been enforced here at all. An
# axiom no reasoner in the pipeline evaluates is documentation, not a
# constraint.
censo:exceeds          owl:propertyDisjointWith censo:possiblyExceeds ,
                                                censo:belowThreshold .
censo:possiblyExceeds  owl:propertyDisjointWith censo:belowThreshold .

censo:ComplianceOutcome a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Compliance outcome"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The verdict of comparing an observation against a threshold.
THREE-valued: the standard is met, it is not met, or compliance cannot be
determined. The three are what the Directive itself admits, and the third is
what closed-world models cannot express. The reason a verdict cannot be
determined is carried by the subclasses of IndeterminateCompliance rather than
by a fourth top value, which would assert a distinction the Directive does not
make."""@en .

censo:Exceedance a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ComplianceOutcome ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
        owl:intersectionOf ( sosa:Observation
                             [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:exceeds ;
                               owl:someValuesFrom censo:Threshold ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Exceedance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Exceedance is established: it holds for every value consistent with the observation."@en .

# MOVED UNDER IndeterminateCompliance, from a fourth top-level value beside
# Exceedance and Compliant.
#
# The arithmetic never agreed with the taxonomy. The headline share of
# assessments this record cannot decide has always been computed as the sum of
# PossibleExceedance, MethodInsufficient, PreconditionUnmet and the rows with no
# bound at all -- 43.8 % of every station-year a European standard reaches. The
# vocabulary said the outcome space was four-valued while the paper's own
# central number counted it as three. One of the two was wrong, and it was the
# vocabulary: "the interval straddles the limit, so the measurement cannot
# decide" IS a compliance that cannot be determined. What distinguishes it from
# the others is the REASON, and a reason is what a subclass carries.
#
# Three top values are then exactly what the Directive asks for -- the standard
# is met, it is not met, or it cannot be determined -- and the subclasses say
# why. A fourth top value would be the vocabulary asserting a distinction the
# Directive does not make.
censo:PossibleExceedance a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:IndeterminateCompliance ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
        owl:intersectionOf ( sosa:Observation
                             [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:possiblyExceeds ;
                               owl:someValuesFrom censo:Threshold ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Possible exceedance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The interval straddles the threshold; the measurement
cannot decide. A subclass of IndeterminateCompliance recording that the reason
is the width of the interval, not a defect of the record: unlike the other
three, this observation was measured, reported and assessable, and the method
still could not answer the question."""@en .

censo:Compliant a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ComplianceOutcome ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
        owl:intersectionOf ( sosa:Observation
                             [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:belowThreshold ;
                               owl:someValuesFrom censo:Threshold ] ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "Compliant"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Compliance is established: it holds for every value consistent with the observation."@en .

censo:IndeterminateCompliance a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:ComplianceOutcome ;
    rdfs:label "Indeterminate compliance"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """No compliance statement is supportable. The third of the
three outcomes the Directive admits, and the only one a two-valued schema has
nowhere to put. It is ABSTRACT in practice: every instance carries one of the
subclasses below, which record WHY no statement is supportable —

(a) NoThresholdDefined  — the regulation sets no limit for this analyte here;
(b) MethodInsufficient  — the observation is censored and the method's LOQ
    exceeds the threshold, so compliance cannot be demonstrated even in
    principle;
(c) PreconditionUnmet   — a precondition is unmet: unknown fraction, missing
    bioavailability covariates, unknown hardness class;
(d) PossibleExceedance  — the result was measured and assessable, and its
    interval straddles the limit, so the method cannot decide either way;
(e) BoundNotEstablished — the record states no bound to compare at all;
(f) CensoredAmbiguous   — censored with the threshold inside [0, LOD], the
    narrower case at the DETECTION limit, which no monitoring release states.

Case (b) is the sharpest: a laboratory can report "not detected" for a substance
whose LOQ is a hundredfold above the legal limit, and a closed-world system
records that as compliance.

PRECEDENCE. A censored result whose LOQ exceeds the threshold has an interval
that straddles it too, so (b) and (d) both describe it. Article 3(3b) is
decisive — the result "shall not be considered" — and such an observation is
recorded under MethodInsufficient. This is a rule-layer priority and NOT an
owl:disjointWith axiom between the two: the same observation may legitimately be
MethodInsufficient against one jurisdiction's standard and PossibleExceedance
against another's, and class-level disjointness would forbid the two-regulation
assessment this vocabulary exists to support. Exclusivity lives where it always
has, on the comparison properties, per observation-threshold pair.

NOTE ON INFERENCE. This class cannot be defined as "neither of the other two",
because under the open-world assumption an unasserted property is not a false
one. It is materialised under a locally closed world; see censo-shapes.ttl."""@en ;
    skos:example "Imidacloprid measured by a method quantifying at 2.065 µg/L against an EU annual-average standard of 0.0068 µg/L: no result the method could return would decide compliance."@en .

censo:MethodInsufficient a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:IndeterminateCompliance ;
    rdfs:label "Method insufficient"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Case (b): the observation is CENSORED and its
limitOfQuantification exceeds the thresholdValue, so Article 3(3b) of Directive
2008/105/EC provides that the result "shall not be considered" for status
assessment. The censoring condition is part of the case: a quantified result
above the standard is evidence of exceedance whatever the method's limit was, and
is not set aside by this provision."""@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Article 3(3b) is written about the limit of
QUANTIFICATION, and this class is scoped to it.

A narrower case exists in principle: a threshold falling below the limit of
DETECTION, where the measurement cannot even establish presence. An earlier
version declared censo:CensoredAmbiguous for it. No European monitoring release
states a detection limit -- WISE-6 carries procedureLOQValue and nothing else --
so no row can be shown to be in that case, and a class no data can reach is a
promise rather than a term. It is retired, on the same rule that retired
censo:ClassBoundaryThreshold and censo:FractionCondition, and the distinction is
recorded here instead: where a release does report a detection limit, the
narrower case is the one where the threshold falls below it, and it is strictly
inside this class."""@en .

censo:PreconditionUnmet a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:IndeterminateCompliance ;
    rdfs:label "Precondition unmet"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Case (c): a required applicability condition is not satisfied."@en .

censo:NoThresholdDefined a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:IndeterminateCompliance ;
    rdfs:label "No threshold defined"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Case (a): the regulation sets no limit for this analyte in this matrix."@en .


# ADDED with the move of PossibleExceedance under this parent. Making the parent
# abstract left one real population with nowhere to go: the 45,467 station-years
# that carry neither a censoring flag nor a limit, 6.5 % of every assessment a
# European standard reaches, which the pipeline had been assigning to the bare
# parent. A class hierarchy whose parent is instantiated directly is a hierarchy
# with a hole in it, and the hole was the largest single reason in the record
# after the two named provisions.
#
# It covers the second, much smaller population for the same reason: a row whose
# value falls below the limit it was measured with contradicts itself, so no
# interval it implies can be trusted either. In both cases what is missing is
# not the verdict but the BOUND the verdict would compare, which is what the
# name says.
censo:BoundNotEstablished a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf censo:IndeterminateCompliance ;
    rdfs:label "Bound not established"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Case (e): the record establishes no bound to compare against
the threshold. Either nothing says whether the number is a measurement — neither
a censoring flag nor a limit is reported, so no interval can be built — or the
number contradicts the limit reported beside it. The distinction from
MethodInsufficient matters: there the bound is known and known to be too wide,
here there is no bound at all, and the two call for different repairs."""@en .

# EXHAUSTIVE: the three outcomes cover ComplianceOutcome, so a verdict is always
# one of them and "nothing recorded" is not a quiet fourth option. Three, not
# four: PossibleExceedance moved under IndeterminateCompliance, where the
# paper's own arithmetic had been counting it all along.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT DISJOINT AT THE CLASS LEVEL. An earlier version asserted
# owl:AllDisjointClasses over these, which made it an inconsistency for a
# single observation to carry two outcomes. That was wrong, and wrong in a way
# that contradicted the vocabulary's own purpose: a compliance outcome is not a
# property of an observation, it is a property of an OBSERVATION-THRESHOLD
# PAIR. One measurement may be compliant against one jurisdiction's standard
# and indeterminate against another's -- the ordinary situation in a graph
# holding two regulation packages, and the thing pluggable regulations exist to
# make expressible. A class-level disjointness axiom forbade it.
#
# Exclusivity is kept where it belongs: the owl:AllDisjointProperties axiom
# over exceeds / possiblyExceeds / belowThreshold, above, makes two verdicts
# about THE SAME pair a contradiction, while leaving different verdicts about
# different thresholds consistent. The outcome logic is therefore still a
# partition -- per observation and threshold, which is the level at which the
# regulation itself is written.
censo:ComplianceOutcome owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
    owl:unionOf ( censo:Exceedance censo:Compliant
                  censo:IndeterminateCompliance ) ] .

# A censored observation cannot establish exceedance: its lower bound is zero,
# so no threshold above zero can be exceeded with certainty. Asserting otherwise
# is a modelling error and this makes it an inconsistency.
censo:CensoredObservation rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Class ; owl:complementOf
      [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:exceeds ;
        owl:someValuesFrom censo:Threshold ] ] .

# An unresolved observation has no detection status, so no verdict may be drawn
# from it at all.
censo:UnresolvedObservation rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Class ; owl:complementOf
      [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty censo:assessableAgainst ;
        owl:someValuesFrom censo:Threshold ] ] .

censo:censoringRecovered a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "censoring recovered"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """True when the detection status was RECONSTRUCTED after the
fact, typically because the source recorded a non-detection as 0 and the status
was recovered by joining the analyte's LOD. Such observations carry an extra
assumption and must remain distinguishable from those whose censoring was
recorded at source."""@en .
